  "worked_solution": "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=a$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=10$\n\n- $Y=b$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4$\n\n- $Y=c$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=c]=6$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]\\cdot P(Y=a)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]\\cdot P(Y=b) \\\\  & \\,+\\text{E}[X∣Y=c]\\cdot P(Y=c) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.5+6\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =6.0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
